WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.248
Imagine you’re a programmer with a
personal assistant that summarizes the top
tech

00:00:05.248 --> 00:00:10.087
news, automatically reads your work
emails, and sorts your tasks every morning
at

00:00:10.087 --> 00:00:10.437
nine.

00:00:10.437 --> 00:00:15.346
Or maybe you’re a salesperson, and it
summarizes your orders and customer
messages

00:00:15.346 --> 00:00:16.257
every evening.

00:00:16.257 --> 00:00:19.483
Or you’re an investor, and this assistant
helps you...

00:00:19.483 --> 00:00:24.881
automatically monitor prices and
proactively send you notifications about
technical

00:00:24.881 --> 00:00:25.537
analysis.

00:00:25.537 --> 00:00:30.424
The special thing about this assistant is
that it can work around the clock, even

00:00:30.424 --> 00:00:31.445
while you sleep.

00:00:31.445 --> 00:00:36.260
You can easily interact with it entirely
through your phone, using messages or

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:36.697
voice.

00:00:36.697 --> 00:00:40.636
And the more you use it, the smarter and
more effective it becomes

00:00:40.636 --> 00:00:43.279
through its automatic learning mechanism.

00:00:43.279 --> 00:00:45.636
This assistant is called Hermes Agent.

00:00:45.636 --> 00:00:49.636
In today’s video, I’ll guide you through
everything from scratch.

00:00:49.636 --> 00:00:54.564
We’ll start by looking at what Hermes
Agent is, how its architecture works, and
how

00:00:54.564 --> 00:00:55.421
to install it.

00:00:55.421 --> 00:00:56.850
Now, let’s get started.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.774
Back at Hotech—I’m Tùng!

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:08.185
For those watching for the first time, I
make videos about programming and

00:01:08.185 --> 00:01:09.038
technology.

00:01:09.038 --> 00:01:11.673
This is the GitHub page for Hermes Agent.

00:01:11.673 --> 00:01:16.246
As you can see, it’s described as an AI
Agent that can learn by itself and

00:01:16.246 --> 00:01:19.346
continuously improve, built by Nous
Research.

00:01:19.346 --> 00:01:23.686
It’s also the only Agent with a feature
called the Learning Loop, or

00:01:23.686 --> 00:01:24.823
the learning loop.

00:01:24.823 --> 00:01:29.515
We’ll look at exactly how the Learning
Loop works later, in the section where we

00:01:29.515 --> 00:01:31.861
explore Hermes Agent’s architecture.

00:01:31.861 --> 00:01:36.766
Basically, it automatically creates skills
from its experience working with us.

00:01:36.766 --> 00:01:41.031
And besides the Learning Loop, it also has
another very useful feature:

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:47.350
it can understand many separate
conversations over time while sharing one
common

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:47.858
memory.

00:01:47.858 --> 00:01:52.795
This is different from how we usually work
with Claude Code, where each session or

00:01:52.795 --> 00:01:57.587
project has its own memory system, so we
can’t know what we discussed in another

00:01:57.587 --> 00:01:58.168
session.

00:01:58.168 --> 00:01:59.001
But

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.580
with Hermes Agent, there’s a system that
can remember all of our conversations and

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:08.287
sessions.

00:02:08.287 --> 00:02:13.472
It only launched this year, in 2026, but
as you can see, Hermes Agent has already

00:02:13.472 --> 00:02:16.457
passed two hundred thousand stars on
GitHub.

00:02:16.457 --> 00:02:19.207
And it’s available under the MIT License.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:24.006
So we can use it completely free of
charge.

00:02:24.006 --> 00:02:29.071
We only pay for the LLM services, meaning
we only pay for the tokens we use.

00:02:29.071 --> 00:02:31.646
The Agent itself is completely free.

00:02:31.646 --> 00:02:34.635
There are several ways to use Hermes
Agent.

00:02:34.635 --> 00:02:37.458
We can run it on a personal laptop, on a
PC,

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:39.780
or on a VPS.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:45.062
But keep in mind that if we want an
assistant that works twenty-four seven,
even

00:02:45.062 --> 00:02:48.343
while we sleep, we should usually host it
on a VPS.

00:02:48.343 --> 00:02:53.625
If we install it on our personal computer,
the computer may go to sleep, and then

00:02:53.625 --> 00:02:55.866
any automatically running tasks

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.102
may stop working.

00:02:59.102 --> 00:03:04.388
So in today’s video, I’ll show you how to
install Hermes Agent on a VPS, giving us

00:03:04.388 --> 00:03:09.275
an assistant that can work for us
twenty-four seven, even while we sleep.

00:03:09.275 --> 00:03:14.722
Simply put, a VPS is like having a
computer running twenty-four seven in the
cloud

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.616
on a cloud system.

00:03:16.616 --> 00:03:22.668
To talk with Hermes Agent, we can easily
use apps such as Telegram, Slack, Discord,

00:03:22.668 --> 00:03:23.501
and more.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.450
Next, let’s look at how Hermes Agent
works.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.856
Here, we have a VPS, essentially a
computer running in the cloud and
operating

00:03:36.856 --> 00:03:38.147
twenty-four seven.

00:03:38.147 --> 00:03:43.084
Once we install Hermes Agent on this VPS,
I’ll show you later in the video how to

00:03:43.084 --> 00:03:44.071
set it up there.

00:03:44.071 --> 00:03:45.362
Once it’s installed

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:56.251
on a VPS, we can easily interact and talk
with Hermes Agent through apps such as

00:03:56.251 --> 00:03:59.582
Telegram, WhatsApp, Discord, Slack, and
more.

00:03:59.582 --> 00:04:04.743
We can use these apps on our phones or an
iPad, and that’s all we need to start

00:04:04.743 --> 00:04:06.658
talking with Hermes Agent.

00:04:11.329 --> 00:04:16.435
Now I’ll walk you through the entire
process, from sending a message in
Telegram to

00:04:16.435 --> 00:04:18.656
seeing how Hermes Agent handles it.

00:04:18.656 --> 00:04:23.466
In our app, when we send a message through
Telegram, Hermes Agent on the VPS has

00:04:23.466 --> 00:04:24.650
something called a

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:31.913
gateway.

00:04:31.913 --> 00:04:34.669
It’s like a door that receives the
message.

00:04:34.669 --> 00:04:39.799
Once it receives that message, it passes
it to a component called the Agent Loop.

00:04:39.799 --> 00:04:45.006
The Agent Loop reads and records the
memories, and it also manages a collection
of

00:04:45.006 --> 00:04:45.925
tools so it can

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:51.287
use them.

00:04:51.287 --> 00:04:56.381
It also has another important job: acting
as the main point of contact with the

00:04:56.381 --> 00:05:00.299
LLMs, or with model providers such as
OpenAI and Anthropic.

00:05:00.299 --> 00:05:03.512
These are the companies providing the AI
models.

00:05:03.512 --> 00:05:06.333
All the parts like the gateway, Agent
Loop,

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:12.149
memory, and tools belong to Hermes Agent.

00:05:12.149 --> 00:05:16.643
The external LLM providers are where we
actually use the AI models.

00:05:16.643 --> 00:05:22.100
So when the Agent Loop receives a request
from the user through apps like these, it

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:25.711
sends a request to the LLMs, to the
external providers

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:29.578
outside.

00:05:29.578 --> 00:05:35.092
When the model replies, if the LLM’s
response says that a tool is also needed,
the

00:05:35.092 --> 00:05:40.853
Agent Loop knows to call the appropriate
tools—for example, to browse the web, open

00:05:40.853 --> 00:05:44.145
a terminal, or use tools for generating
images.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:50.336
And this is what we call the Harness—the
part that wraps around a model, which I

00:05:50.336 --> 00:05:52.269
introduced in an earlier video.

00:05:52.269 --> 00:05:55.994
If you'd like, you can go back and watch
my video about Harness.

00:05:55.994 --> 00:06:00.864
Once it finishes running the tools, it
remembers the relevant memories, and then

00:06:00.864 --> 00:06:02.082
sends its response.

00:06:03.392 --> 00:06:05.325
This is our Telegram message.

00:06:05.325 --> 00:06:10.350
So, this is the complete lifecycle of a
message when we work with Hermes Agent.

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:15.375
Now we'll go into more detail to see how
it stores memory, and how it creates and

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:16.457
updates skills.

00:06:16.457 --> 00:06:17.290
So now we'll

00:06:21.972 --> 00:06:24.608
describe how its self-learning loop works.

00:06:24.608 --> 00:06:28.811
Suppose it's working and notices that a
certain task keeps recurring.

00:06:28.811 --> 00:06:33.656
At that point, it automatically recognizes
the pattern and writes the necessary

00:06:33.656 --> 00:06:37.005
skills itself, so we don't have to create
them manually.

00:06:37.005 --> 00:06:40.567
Of course, you can still create skills
manually if you want.

00:06:40.567 --> 00:06:42.063
But because Hermes Agent

00:06:42.063 --> 00:06:47.021
comes with the ability to learn on its
own, create skills, and update existing

00:06:47.021 --> 00:06:51.216
skills, it becomes smarter over time and
works more efficiently.

00:06:51.216 --> 00:06:56.327
It's just like an employee: when someone
is new to a job, they will naturally take

00:06:56.327 --> 00:06:59.988
longer than someone with five or ten years
of experience.

00:06:59.988 --> 00:07:00.675
This agent

00:07:00.675 --> 00:07:02.796
now has that same ability to learn.

00:07:02.796 --> 00:07:07.697
Whenever it encounters a similar task
again, it can use the skills it remembered

00:07:07.697 --> 00:07:08.502
from before.

00:07:08.502 --> 00:07:12.525
So it only needs to retrieve that skill
and apply it in the same way.

00:07:12.525 --> 00:07:17.572
As a result, both its working time and the
quality of its work improve compared with

00:07:17.572 --> 00:07:18.742
the first attempt.

00:07:18.742 --> 00:07:21.668
That's the self-learning loop in Hermes
Agent.

00:07:21.668 --> 00:07:26.432
For example, the first time it creates a
skill, but the second time, the time it

00:07:26.432 --> 00:07:31.342
takes to complete the task drops, and the
number of tokens it consumes also drops

00:07:31.342 --> 00:07:32.368
significantly.

00:07:32.368 --> 00:07:37.425
This is one of the biggest strengths of
Hermes Agent: its self-learning loop, also

00:07:37.425 --> 00:07:39.037
called its learning loop.

00:07:39.037 --> 00:07:41.749
If you're not yet clear on what this skill
is,

00:07:41.749 --> 00:07:44.303
you can watch my video about agent skills.

00:07:44.303 --> 00:07:48.828
Through this learning loop, as it creates
skills like these, it builds up a

00:07:48.828 --> 00:07:50.214
collection of skills.

00:07:50.214 --> 00:07:55.177
If you've watched my video about agent
skills, you also know that a simple skill
is

00:07:55.177 --> 00:07:57.658
just a Markdown file, or simply a piece of

00:07:57.658 --> 00:07:58.478
basic text.

00:07:58.478 --> 00:08:03.892
It has a section enclosed by three dashes,
like this, and that section is called

00:08:03.892 --> 00:08:04.876
frontmatter.

00:08:04.876 --> 00:08:10.536
The reason this works better than using
rules is that, by default, a rules file
will

00:08:10.536 --> 00:08:11.369
usually be

00:08:15.532 --> 00:08:20.881
loaded in its entirety into the context
window, regardless of its length, and it

00:08:20.881 --> 00:08:23.755
occupies a certain number of tokens there.

00:08:23.755 --> 00:08:25.512
Skills work differently.

00:08:25.512 --> 00:08:30.541
Even if we have a large collection of
skills, we can think of each one as a work

00:08:30.541 --> 00:08:35.012
skill—for example, the skill used to
export a PDF file, or the skill

00:08:35.012 --> 00:08:40.411
used to convert a piece of text into a
speakable file, or text to speech, or to

00:08:40.411 --> 00:08:42.725
convert an audio file into text.

00:08:42.725 --> 00:08:44.953
Each of these is called a skill.

00:08:44.953 --> 00:08:50.438
It's just like us: we can have many
skills, such as working with Excel, giving

00:08:50.438 --> 00:08:52.323
presentations, and so on.

00:08:52.323 --> 00:08:53.156
So now

00:08:54.212 --> 00:08:56.763
agents are equipped with skills as well.

00:08:56.763 --> 00:09:01.864
With Hermes Agent, there may be many
skills, and it can automatically create
many

00:09:01.864 --> 00:09:06.965
more, but it still works efficiently
because, although there are lots of
skills,

00:09:06.965 --> 00:09:09.741
the amount of text it places into the
context

00:09:12.412 --> 00:09:14.261
window is actually very small.

00:09:14.261 --> 00:09:19.309
That's because it only sends in this
frontmatter section, the part between
these two

00:09:19.309 --> 00:09:20.233
sets of dashes.

00:09:20.233 --> 00:09:25.436
The specific content of the skill isn't
placed into the context window when we
first

00:09:25.436 --> 00:09:26.699
start a new session.

00:09:26.699 --> 00:09:30.489
So the number of tokens it occupies there
is extremely small.

00:09:30.489 --> 00:09:33.537
Think of the left side here as a table of
contents.

00:09:33.537 --> 00:09:37.327
The agent only reads these headings and
their descriptions,

00:09:39.412 --> 00:09:42.374
then checks whether they match the user's
request.

00:09:45.292 --> 00:09:50.224
If it finds a match, only then does it
read the entire skill and load it into the

00:09:50.224 --> 00:09:51.303
context window.

00:09:51.303 --> 00:09:55.928
So even with many skills, these agents can
still work very efficiently.

00:09:55.928 --> 00:09:59.473
Now let's look at the memory structure of
Hermes Agent.

00:09:59.473 --> 00:10:01.785
During its operation, Hermes Agent

00:10:04.892 --> 00:10:09.571
as we chat with it, through messages,
images, or files that we provide,

00:10:09.571 --> 00:10:15.042
automatically creates files for us, such
as the user file, the memory file, and the

00:10:15.042 --> 00:10:16.311
file called sound.

00:10:16.311 --> 00:10:20.117
These contain information it has compiled
and keeps very

00:10:23.552 --> 00:10:27.014
briefly: who we are and who uses the
agent.

00:10:27.932 --> 00:10:31.673
In my own experience, it only takes up
around 10 kilobytes.

00:10:31.673 --> 00:10:37.093
Every conversation is placed into that
conversation's context window when we
first

00:10:37.093 --> 00:10:37.703
start it.

00:10:37.703 --> 00:10:43.200
User contains user information, while
memory contains some summarized details
that

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:45.566
it has automatically compiled using

00:10:46.052 --> 00:10:46.604
the LLM.

00:10:46.604 --> 00:10:49.364
Sound describes the user's personality.

00:10:49.364 --> 00:10:53.701
These three files are always loaded into
every new conversation.

00:10:53.701 --> 00:10:55.278
That's the first layer.

00:10:55.278 --> 00:10:59.851
The second layer is where it stores all
the conversations we have with

00:11:03.532 --> 00:11:04.460
Hermes Agent.

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:06.317
It uses SQLite at this layer.

00:11:06.317 --> 00:11:11.269
Since the amount of data can become very
large, it doesn't automatically put

00:11:11.269 --> 00:11:15.292
everything into the context window when we
open a new session.

00:11:15.292 --> 00:11:18.232
Instead, it only looks things up when
needed.

00:11:18.232 --> 00:11:22.101
Finally, the last layer can be expanded
through additional

00:11:23.112 --> 00:11:28.182
For third-party memory, we have a range of
tools such as memzero, Hisight, and

00:11:28.182 --> 00:11:29.040
Overviking.

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:34.344
There are many external memory providers
like these, and we can configure Hermes

00:11:34.344 --> 00:11:35.514
Agent to use them.

00:11:35.514 --> 00:11:38.322
In terms of memory, there are three
layers.

00:11:38.322 --> 00:11:39.155
First—

00:11:42.292 --> 00:11:46.327
And that is the overall architecture I
wanted to share with you.

